  Here's a possible help.  Way back when I used a trick with record
structures to avoid saving out meta-data cruft that might not be
compatible across multiple versions and/or compilers.

  Add a union to each record structure to be saved.  The new union
will be an alternate access structure that will consist of a single
array of char (or int, or whatever works).

  This should provide a way of saving just the data in the record
without adding (possibly) incompatible meta data.

  My recollection of this is very rusty, but something like:


  If the current record structure is named record_struct, then:

union {
  unsigned char save_array[sizeof(record_struct)];
  struct record_struct;
} record_struct_save;

  Use record_struct_save.record_struct to access the data for use.
Use record_struct_save.save_array to save and restore the data to and
from a file.

  Hopefully each compiler will be able to convert the data to and from
the union.  

  This is just a general idea of the technique.  It's been quite a
while since I used it.  My C language syntax is also not to be
trusted.

  This idea first came to me sometime back when trying to modify a
save file directly, as a prelude to parsing the file for portability.
Strange things happened.  Sometimes a field didn't seem to be present
in the save file unless it was non-zero.  My best guess it that fields
with a value of zero were being stored indirectly as meta-data.  This
approach may force direct writing of the data.




